<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Most "workflow" requests are a single approval gate. `ash_bpmn` ships two layers, and the first stands alone: `AshBpmn.Changes.RequireApproval` on an action. No diagram. Reach for BPMN (lesson 21) only when routing, parallelism or joins appear.

You get: a human task with assignee, due date and outcome; candidates **materialized as rows**; maker-checker applied when candidates are resolved; delegation; remind/escalate/expire timers as Oban jobs that are cancelled when the task completes; and a process event log an auditor can read.

## The maker-checker pair

The gate will name the role `refund_approver`, and the host `AssignmentResolver` turns that clause into candidate rows by filtering `AshEnterprise.Security.UserRole` on `role.name == "refund_approver"` (`lib/ash_enterprise/process/assignment_resolver.ex`). The seeds create one administrator and no such role, so build the pair yourself — a role with exactly that name, a second user to hold it, and the assignment, in the same shape the seeder uses. Candidates are materialized **when the task is created**, so this has to exist before you propose anything.

```elixir
require Ash.Query

maker = Ash.read_first!(AshEnterprise.Accounts.User, authorize?: false)
tenant = maker.organization_id
root = maker.owning_business_unit_id

role =
  AshEnterprise.Security.Role
  |> Ash.Query.filter(name == "refund_approver")
  |> Ash.read_one!(actor: maker, tenant: tenant) ||
    AshEnterprise.Security.Role
    |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:create, %{name: "refund_approver", owning_business_unit_id: root},
      actor: maker, tenant: tenant
    )
    |> Ash.create!()

role.name
```

```elixir
checker =
  AshEnterprise.Accounts.User
  |> Ash.Query.filter(email == "checker@example.com")
  |> Ash.read_one!(authorize?: false) ||
    AshEnterprise.Accounts.User
    |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:register_with_password,
      %{email: "checker@example.com", password: "password1234", password_confirmation: "password1234"},
      authorize?: false
    )
    |> Ash.create!()

checker =
  checker
  |> Ash.Changeset.for_update(:assign_to_business_unit, %{owning_business_unit_id: root},
    actor: maker, tenant: tenant
  )
  |> Ash.update!()

AshEnterprise.Security.UserRole
|> Ash.Query.filter(user_id == ^checker.id and role_id == ^role.id)
|> Ash.read_one!(actor: maker, tenant: tenant) ||
  AshEnterprise.Security.UserRole
  |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:assign,
    %{user_id: checker.id, role_id: role.id, scoping_business_unit_id: root},
    actor: maker, tenant: tenant
  )
  |> Ash.create!()

"#{checker.email} holds #{role.name}"
```

The name must be exactly `"refund_approver"`: the resolver matches on it, and an almost-right name resolves to nobody — a task nobody can claim, which presents as a silent stall. (`authorize?: false` on registration is the seeder's own path: registering a user is an authentication action, not a tenant write.)

## Gate the refund

Change the Refund so that proposing it opens an approval, and approval runs `:approve`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
create :propose do
  accept [:ticket_ref, :amount, :reason]

  change AshBpmn.Changes.RequireApproval,
    key: "refund.approve",
    name: "Approve refund",
    outcomes: [:approved, :rejected],
    candidates: [{:role, "refund_approver"}],     # the host AssignmentResolver interprets these
    excluding: ["subject.created_by_id"],         # maker-checker, by subtraction
    on_complete: %{approved: "approve", rejected: "reject"},
    escalate_in: [hours: 24],
    expire_in: [days: 7]
end
```

Three things to internalize:

1. **Maker-checker is never a `forbid_if`.** It is `excluding:`, applied when candidate rows are built. A policy that forbids the maker is evaluated per row per request; a subtraction is done once, and the task list stays one indexed query.
2. **`on_complete` names actions — through an allowlist.** Actions are run by the host's `AshBpmn.ActionInvoker`, which in this app is a **closed, reviewed list in code** (`lib/ash_enterprise/process/action_invoker.ex`): a diagram is tenant-authored input, so it may only name refs the host registered, and publish-time `exists?/1` refuses anything else. Your `validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)` from lesson 15 still guards `:approve`, for this caller and every other.
3. **The rule "who approves" is not here.** Today it is a fixed role. In lesson 21 it comes from the `refund_approver` decision.

## Register the on_complete refs

`approve` and `reject` are not in the invoker's registry yet — without them, the task completes and the refund quietly stays `:proposed`. Register both, the way the invoker's own error message tells you to:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/process/action_invoker.ex

# Inside @registry, beside the existing entries:
"approve" => %{
  run: &__MODULE__.approve_refund_action/1,
  action: {AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :approve}
},
"reject" => %{
  run: &__MODULE__.reject_refund_action/1,
  action: {AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :reject}
}

# ...and the two functions, beside record_risk/1 and its siblings:
@doc "Runs the outcome the task was decided with, as the process system actor."
def approve_refund_action(ctx) do
  AshEnterprise.Tutorial.approve_refund!(ctx[:subject], AshEnterprise.Process.EngineContext.engine_opts(ctx))
  :ok
rescue
  e -> {:error, Exception.message(e)}
end

@doc "Runs the outcome the task was decided with, as the process system actor."
def reject_refund_action(ctx) do
  AshEnterprise.Tutorial.reject_refund!(ctx[:subject], AshEnterprise.Process.EngineContext.engine_opts(ctx))
  :ok
rescue
  e -> {:error, Exception.message(e)}
end
```

The named function is what execution calls — every registry entry carries a capture like `&__MODULE__.record_risk/1`, because an anonymous function body stored in a module attribute cannot be injected back into the code that reads the attribute, so closures never appear in `@registry`. `engine_opts/1` swaps the engine's own actor — a struct your policies have never heard of, so a host action called with it is simply forbidden — for `AshEnterprise.Platform.SystemActor.process()`, and carries the tenant from the subject. The `action:` tuple is what the designer's panel introspects, so the catalogue cannot drift from what runs. `recompile()` in the attached node.

## Work the task

Propose a refund as one user, list the tasks of a second user with `AshBpmn.my_tasks/2`, claim it, and decide `:approved`. Then confirm the proposer cannot see the task at all.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  refund = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.propose_refund!("T-2001", Decimal.new("300"), actor: maker, tenant: tenant)

  [task | _] =
    AshBpmn.my_tasks(AshEnterprise.Bpmn, principal_ids: [checker.id], actor: checker, tenant: tenant)
  task = AshBpmn.claim_task!(task, actor: checker, tenant: tenant)
  AshBpmn.decide!(task, outcome: :approved, actor: checker, tenant: tenant)

  AshBpmn.my_tasks(AshEnterprise.Bpmn, principal_ids: [maker.id], actor: maker, tenant: tenant)  # no refund task
  Ash.reload!(refund, actor: checker, tenant: tenant).status                                     # :approved
  ```

  The task list is one indexed query over candidate rows, so `my_tasks/2` wants the actor's **principal ids**, not just the actor — check the exact options with `h AshBpmn.my_tasks/2`, and the same for `decide/2`. If the maker *does* see the task, the bug is in candidate resolution, so debug `excluding:` and the host `AssignmentResolver`, not policies.

  </div>
</details>

## Timers must be cancellable

Look at the Oban jobs for the task before and after deciding. The escalation job must be gone afterwards. If you ever add another completion path, cancel the timers there too: an escalation email for a refund approved four days ago is the canonical incident.

```elixir
import Ecto.Query
AshEnterprise.Repo.all(from j in Oban.Job, where: j.state in ["scheduled", "available"], select: {j.worker, j.args})
```

When you are finished with the whole tutorial, the overview's **When you're done** recipe reverts the invoker registry edit — the one tracked file this lesson touches — along with everything else.

